### What is the digit-only check?

The digit-only check verifies that every character in a string is a decimal digit from zero through nine, with no letters, spaces, punctuation, or symbols anywhere. In a banking application, this validation runs on account numbers, PINs, OTPs, and monetary amount fields before they reach any parser or database query.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

public class DigitChecker {
    public static boolean containsOnlyDigits(String input) {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
            if (!Character.isDigit(input.charAt(i))) {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }
}
```

### Why does this matter in banking?

Account numbers, IFSC codes, and transaction amounts are all expected to be numeric or follow strict patterns. If a stray space or letter slips through, a database lookup fails silently or a transfer routes to the wrong account. Rajesh at the National Bank of India validates every numeric field at the entry point so downstream services never receive malformed data.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.validation.DigitChecker;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class DepositService {
    public BigDecimal parseAmount(String raw) {
        if (!DigitChecker.containsOnlyDigits(raw)) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException(
                "Amount must contain only digits: " + raw);
        }
        return new BigDecimal(raw);
    }
}
```

### How does the fail-fast loop work internally?

The loop starts at index zero and advances one character at a time. The moment `Character.isDigit` returns false, the method returns false immediately without scanning the rest of the string. This fail-fast behavior means that for invalid input, the check stops at the first bad character, which is efficient when most rejections happen early.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

public class FailFastDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String accountNumber = "2001X456";
        for (int i = 0; i < accountNumber.length(); i++) {
            char c = accountNumber.charAt(i);
            if (!Character.isDigit(c)) {
                System.out.println("Non-digit found at index " + i + ": '" + c + "'");
                break;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Non-digit found at index 4: 'X'
```

### What are the edge cases?

The most common edge case is the empty string. A naive loop finishes immediately and returns true, but an empty field is not valid numeric input. Null input would throw a NullPointerException if not guarded. A string with leading or trailing spaces like " 123 " also fails because spaces are not digits.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

public class EdgeCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(DigitChecker.containsOnlyDigits(null));     // false
        System.out.println(DigitChecker.containsOnlyDigits(""));       // false
        System.out.println(DigitChecker.containsOnlyDigits(" 123 ")); // false
        System.out.println(DigitChecker.containsOnlyDigits("00000")); // true
        System.out.println(DigitChecker.containsOnlyDigits("9"));     // true
    }
}
```

```text
false
false
false
true
true
```

### What is the Unicode trap with Character.isDigit()?

`Character.isDigit()` is Unicode-aware. It returns true for digit characters in many scripts, not just ASCII zero through nine. For example, Devanagari digits (used in Hindi) and Arabic-Indic digits all pass the check. This means a field validated with `Character.isDigit()` might accept characters that `Integer.parseInt()` or `BigDecimal` cannot parse, causing a downstream `NumberFormatException`.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

public class UnicodeDigitDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        char devanagariOne = '\u0967'; // Devanagari digit 1
        System.out.println("isDigit: " + Character.isDigit(devanagariOne)); // true

        // But parseInt cannot handle it
        try {
            Integer.parseInt(String.valueOf(devanagariOne));
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            System.out.println("parseInt failed: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
isDigit: true
parseInt failed: For input string: "\u0967"
```

### How do you enforce strict ASCII-only digits?

For banking fields that must contain only ASCII digits, compare each character against the range from `'0'` to `'9'` instead of calling `Character.isDigit()`. This rejects all non-ASCII digit characters while keeping the same fail-fast loop structure.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

public class AsciiDigitChecker {
    public static boolean isAsciiDigitsOnly(String input) {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }
        for (int i = 0; i < input.length(); i++) {
            char c = input.charAt(i);
            if (c < '0' || c > '9') {
                return false;
            }
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isAsciiDigitsOnly("200145"));  // true
        System.out.println(isAsciiDigitsOnly("\u0967")); // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```

### How does the regex alternative compare?

A regular expression like `str.matches("[0-9]+")` checks the same condition in one line. However, `String.matches()` compiles the pattern on every call, which adds overhead on hot paths. For infrequent validation, regex is fine. For high-volume batch processing, pre-compile the pattern or use the explicit loop.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

import java.util.regex.Pattern;

public class RegexDigitChecker {
    private static final Pattern DIGITS_ONLY = Pattern.compile("[0-9]+");

    public static boolean check(String input) {
        if (input == null || input.isEmpty()) {
            return false;
        }
        return DIGITS_ONLY.matcher(input).matches();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(check("200145"));  // true
        System.out.println(check("20 01"));   // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting the empty-string guard. A second mistake is using `Character.isDigit()` when strict ASCII is required, which silently accepts non-ASCII digits. A third mistake is assuming that all-digit means parseable into an `int` or `long`, when the value might be a 20-digit account number that overflows.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

public class CommonMistakesDemo {
    // WRONG: no null/empty guard, uses isDigit for ASCII field
    public static boolean wrongCheck(String s) {
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            if (!Character.isDigit(s.charAt(i))) return false;
        }
        return true; // empty string returns true incorrectly
    }

    // CORRECT: guards null/empty, uses ASCII range
    public static boolean correctCheck(String s) {
        if (s == null || s.isEmpty()) return false;
        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            if (c < '0' || c > '9') return false;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Wrong on empty: " + wrongCheck(""));       // true (bug)
        System.out.println("Correct on empty: " + correctCheck("")); // false
    }
}
```

```text
Wrong on empty: true
Correct on empty: false
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

The loop runs in O(n) time where n is the length of the string. It uses O(1) extra space because no additional data structures are created. The regex approach also runs in O(n) time but has higher constant overhead from pattern matching. For batch validation of millions of account numbers, the loop approach is measurably faster.

```java
package com.bank.validation;

public class PerformanceDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String largeNumber = "1".repeat(1_000_000);
        long start = System.nanoTime();
        boolean result = AsciiDigitChecker.isAsciiDigitsOnly(largeNumber);
        long elapsed = System.nanoTime() - start;
        System.out.println("Result: " + result + ", Time: " + elapsed / 1_000 + " microseconds");
    }
}
```

